{"name":"Cathay Spice","hex":"#9d632d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Aromatic or pungent plant matter (usually dried) used to season or flavor food.","The quality of being spicy."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add spice or spices to; season.","To spice up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spice"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cathay","blocks":[{"text":"Cathay is a historical name for China that was used in Europe. During the early modern period, the term Cathay initially evolved as a term referring to what is now Northern China, completely separate and distinct from China, which was a reference to southern China. As knowledge of East Asia increased, Cathay came to be seen as the same polity as China as a whole. The term Cathay became a poetic name for China."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Empire_of_the_Great_Khan_%28Catayo%29_according_to_the_Catalan_Atlas_%281375%29.jpg/330px-Empire_of_the_Great_Khan_%28Catayo%29_according_to_the_Catalan_Atlas_%281375%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathay","wikidata":"Q1050868"},{"title":"Spice","blocks":[{"text":"In the culinary arts, a spice is a seed, fruit, root, bark, or other plant substance primarily used for flavouring or colouring food. Spices are distinguished from herbs, which are the leaves, flowers, or stems of plants used for flavouring or as a garnish. Spices and herbs are both seasonings. \nSpices are sometimes used in medicine, religious rituals, cosmetics, or perfume production. They are usually classified into spices, spice seeds, and herbal categories. For example, vanilla is commonly used as an ingredient in fragrance manufacturing. Plant-based sweeteners such as sugar are not considered spices."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Spices1.jpg/330px-Spices1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spice","wikidata":"Q42527"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/CEM-09-Asiae-Nova-Descriptio-China-2510.jpg/500px-CEM-09-Asiae-Nova-Descriptio-China-2510.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/CEM-09-Asiae-Nova-Descriptio-China-2510.jpg/1280px-CEM-09-Asiae-Nova-Descriptio-China-2510.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/CEM-09-Asiae-Nova-Descriptio-China-2510.jpg/500px-CEM-09-Asiae-Nova-Descriptio-China-2510.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"On this 1570 map by Abraham Ortelius, Cataio is located inland of China (referring to today's Guangdong) and Chequan (Zhejiang), and borders on \"Thebet\" in the southwest and \"Camul\" in the west. There is also Mangi (between \"Cataio\" and  Xanton (Shandong)). The objects in \"Cataio\" are based on Marco Polo's description and include the capital Cambalu, Xandu, and a marble bridge.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACEM-09-Asiae-Nova-Descriptio-China-2510.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/CEM-15-Asia-Mercator-1595-Tartaria-2532.jpg/500px-CEM-15-Asia-Mercator-1595-Tartaria-2532.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/CEM-15-Asia-Mercator-1595-Tartaria-2532.jpg/1280px-CEM-15-Asia-Mercator-1595-Tartaria-2532.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/CEM-15-Asia-Mercator-1595-Tartaria-2532.jpg/500px-CEM-15-Asia-Mercator-1595-Tartaria-2532.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gerardus Mercator's placing of the \"Kingdom of Cathay\" (Cathay Reg.) on the Pacific Coast north-east of China remained typical for a number of maps published in the decades to follow (originally published 1595)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACEM-15-Asia-Mercator-1595-Tartaria-2532.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/CEM-19-Asiae-nova-description-1610-Jodocus-Hondius-2538.jpg/500px-CEM-19-Asiae-nova-description-1610-Jodocus-Hondius-2538.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/CEM-19-Asiae-nova-description-1610-Jodocus-Hondius-2538.jpg/1280px-CEM-19-Asiae-nova-description-1610-Jodocus-Hondius-2538.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/CEM-19-Asiae-nova-description-1610-Jodocus-Hondius-2538.jpg/500px-CEM-19-Asiae-nova-description-1610-Jodocus-Hondius-2538.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A typical early 17th-century depiction of \"Cataia\" as China's northeastern neighbour, Jodocus Hondius, 1610","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACEM-19-Asiae-nova-description-1610-Jodocus-Hondius-2538.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/John-Speed-The-Kingdome-of-China-1626-2544.jpg/500px-John-Speed-The-Kingdome-of-China-1626-2544.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/John-Speed-The-Kingdome-of-China-1626-2544.jpg/1280px-John-Speed-The-Kingdome-of-China-1626-2544.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/John-Speed-The-Kingdome-of-China-1626-2544.jpg/500px-John-Speed-The-Kingdome-of-China-1626-2544.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Not convinced by the Jesuits, John Speed in 1626 follows Jodocus Hondius' layout: He shows the chain of Silk Road cities visited by de Góis (Cuchia, Chialis, Turfan, Camul) – but has it directed not toward China's Shaanxi Sancii, as shown by de Góis, but toward the mysterious \"Cathaya, the Chief Kingdome of Great Cam\", northeast of China. Naturally, Cambalu and Xandu are shown in Cathay, while Shuntien (Beijing) is in China.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn-Speed-The-Kingdome-of-China-1626-2544.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/CEM-36-Regno-della-China-2355.jpg/500px-CEM-36-Regno-della-China-2355.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/CEM-36-Regno-della-China-2355.jpg/1280px-CEM-36-Regno-della-China-2355.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/CEM-36-Regno-della-China-2355.jpg/500px-CEM-36-Regno-della-China-2355.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cathay is finally gone: This 1689 map by Giacomo Cantelli da Vignola from Modena identifies it (and Mangi) with China.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACEM-36-Regno-della-China-2355.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Indianspicesherbs.jpg/500px-Indianspicesherbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Indianspicesherbs.jpg/1280px-Indianspicesherbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Indianspicesherbs.jpg/500px-Indianspicesherbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A group of Indian herbs and spices in bowls","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndianspicesherbs.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Spices_of_Sa%C3%BAde_flea_market%2C_S%C3%A3o_Paulo%2C_Brazil.jpg/500px-Spices_of_Sa%C3%BAde_flea_market%2C_S%C3%A3o_Paulo%2C_Brazil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Spices_of_Sa%C3%BAde_flea_market%2C_S%C3%A3o_Paulo%2C_Brazil.jpg/1280px-Spices_of_Sa%C3%BAde_flea_market%2C_S%C3%A3o_Paulo%2C_Brazil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Spices_of_Sa%C3%BAde_flea_market%2C_S%C3%A3o_Paulo%2C_Brazil.jpg/500px-Spices_of_Sa%C3%BAde_flea_market%2C_S%C3%A3o_Paulo%2C_Brazil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spices of Saúde flea market, São Paulo, Brazil","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpices_of_Sa%C3%BAde_flea_market%2C_S%C3%A3o_Paulo%2C_Brazil.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Le_livre_des_merveilles_de_Marco_Polo-pepper.jpg/500px-Le_livre_des_merveilles_de_Marco_Polo-pepper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Le_livre_des_merveilles_de_Marco_Polo-pepper.jpg/1280px-Le_livre_des_merveilles_de_Marco_Polo-pepper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Le_livre_des_merveilles_de_Marco_Polo-pepper.jpg/500px-Le_livre_des_merveilles_de_Marco_Polo-pepper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"The Mullus\" harvesting pepper. Illustration from a French edition of The Travels of Marco Polo.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALe_livre_des_merveilles_de_Marco_Polo-pepper.jpg"